WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.220
Many Node.js teams use external identity providers and assume authentication has been outsourced.

2
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:14.120
In reality, the provider handles part of the flow, while the application still owns important security decisions.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:19.500
OAuth 2.0 is primarily an authorization framework.

4
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.200
OpenID Connect adds an identity layer for authentication.

5
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:34.020
The application should understand whether it is validating an ID token, using an access token, exchanging an authorization code, or establishing a local session after a provider login.

6
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:42.300
Modern interactive flows commonly use Authorization Code Flow with PKCE, but the durable lesson is validation and clear responsibility.

7
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:53.920
Redirect URI validation, state, nonce, issuer validation, audience validation, token handling, and provider metadata are security-critical details.

8
00:00:54.020 --> 00:01:03.380
These protocols should be implemented through well-maintained libraries and provider configuration, not hand-rolled request parsing and custom assumptions.

9
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.880
Protocol correctness is difficult and easy to get subtly wrong.

10
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:16.080
External identity does not remove local account mapping, authorization, session handling, logging, or account lifecycle decisions.

11
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:32.820
After sign-in, the application still decides which local account is associated with the external identity, whether the account is enabled, which tenant or organization applies, what roles or permissions are current, and whether step-up authentication is needed for sensitive actions.

12
00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:39.160
Signing in through a provider is the beginning of local trust decisions, not the end.
